 "result": "I ran the suite: 163 tests, 161 passed, 1 failed and 1 errored. All 160 `test_many` cases and `test_pricing.test_no_discount` passed.\n\n**1. `test_parse.TestParse.test_parse_amount`: ERROR (an exception was raised, not a failed assertion)**\n```\nFile \"tests\\test_parse.py\", line 7, in test_parse_amount\n    self.assertEqual(parse_amount(\"1,234.50\"), 1234.5)\nFile \"calc\\parse.py\", line 5, in parse_amount\n    return float(text.strip())\nValueError: could not convert string to float: '1,234.50'\n```\n`parse_amount` passes the string straight to `float()`, which can't handle the thousands separator (`,`).\n\n**2. `test_pricing.TestPricing.test_discount_rounding`: FAIL**\n```\nFile \"tests\\test_pricing.py\", line 7, in test_discount_rounding\n    self.assertEqual(apply_discount(2.30, 50), 1.15)\nAssertionError: 1.14 != 1.15\n```\n`apply_discount(2.30, 50)` returned `1.14` where the test expects `1.15`. That points to a rounding problem: 2.30 \u00c3\u2014 0.5 in binary floating point comes out just under 1.15, which then rounds down. I didn't open `calc/pricing.py`, so I haven't confirmed exactly how it rounds.\n\nI only ran the tests and didn't change anything.",
